"""Content D: Liminal Places, Runes, Staves, Charms, Ancestors, Oath."""

CH7 = [
("lore", "Liminal Places", "Wells and the gifts left in them",
 ["Bog water and spring water have kept things for a very long time. Long ago, people laid swords, cauldrons, and brooches in lakes, bogs, and springs. Diggers still find them where rivers slow and springs rise. Scholars call these gifts votive deposits. That means objects given on purpose to a place. The custom did not fully die out. At holy wells in the northern and western lands, people still walk a circle (called a round) around the water and tie cloth to a tree or post above it. Some old gifts went in whole and some did not. What mattered most was the giving itself.",
  "A well is a place with its own history. It is not a machine that grants wishes. Old deposits are well documented: they were found, lifted from the ground, and recorded. Rounds and cloth trees are living folk customs, kept by families who look after each site. Tossing coins into a living spring is a newer habit of visitors, and coins can pollute the water. Never drink wild water, because you cannot know what is in it.",
  "When you visit, walk the round as it is kept at each place. Where no round is marked, walk once around sunwise, meaning clockwise in the northern half of the world. Tie only cloth that will rot away, such as natural cotton or wool. Ask leave before you lift any water, and sit a while to listen before you leave. Where the well is built over, stand at the nearest marked spot instead. The visit takes under an hour. Take nothing away from the site, and leave only what you chose to give. Keep any promise you made to the place.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Old gifts in lakes and springs are found by archaeology. Rounds and cloth trees are living folk custom."],
 "Name your nearest spring or well. Give the day you will go, the round you will walk, and what you will leave there. Write it in three dated lines."),
("lore", "Liminal Places", "Sea roads",
 ["In Icelandic sagas such as Egils saga, the sea is a road between farms that could never meet by land. Sailors had to follow its rules. Some Icelandic sagas show voyages that begin with vows and end with thanks. Some mention gifts to the sea and wind. The crew keeps to its place, and the oars are stowed in order. A shore belongs to neither farm. Storytellers use open water as the setting for hard bargains and farewells. Promises made on the crossing are held to account, meaning people check whether they were kept.",
  "Mid-voyage has its own rule. No shore owns the middle of the water, so every promise made there must be said out loud and kept. Icelandic sagas show crews naming what they leave and what they carry. They speak their terms aloud while still on the water. To reach the far bank, say your terms plainly and keep them.",
  "Many people now live with an unfinished move, a half-paid house, or a task that was never finished. The old idea of a crossing still helps. Every crossing has a bank behind you and a bank ahead. On each crossing, choose one thing to carry over and one thing to set down. Write the marker on a slip of paper, and say the words aloud. Nothing needs to be burned or buried. The crossing is finished when you keep the terms you set.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Sea-voyage vows appear in Icelandic sagas as a story pattern."],
 "Name the crossing you are in now. Name the bank behind you and the bank ahead. Write what you will carry over and what you will set down. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Liminal Places", "Doorways you use every day",
 ["Every doorway is an edge between outside and inside. Folk custom treats it that way. People have put juniper sprigs above the lintel, which is the beam over the door, and a pinch of salt along the sill. A verse is said when going out, and a breath is taken when coming home. You touch the door frame many times a day without noticing. This habit asks you to notice it once.",
  "That doorway marks the edge of your home, the way a riverbank marks the edge of a river. Small, steady care keeps the inside calm. Juniper, salt, and short verses are folk survivals (old habits that lasted in ordinary households) from many districts, not one single ritual. A calendar can turn these scraps into a habit you repeat.",
  "Start with the door you use most. Sweep the sill and clear the corners. A store-bought sprig of green is enough. Use one short line for leaving and one for coming home. Say them every day for a month. Doing it every day matters more than doing it grandly. Your hands and your voice do the work. A door kept with care helps the whole house feel settled.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Threshold habits come from folk custom in many districts."],
 "Choose the doorway you use most. Clean it and add a green sprig in fifteen minutes. Write your two short lines and today’s date. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Liminal Places", "Dawn, dusk, and Yule night",
 ["Twice a day, the light is neither fully day nor fully night. Once a year, at Yule (midwinter), the season does something similar. In Icelandic folk belief, the mounds stood open on Yule night and the dead could walk near the living. Dawn and dusk carry a smaller daily version of that opening. The house is quiet, and the edges show clearly. Birds wake or settle as the light shifts. Even a city window shows the change.",
  "In old stories, edges were meeting times. Bargains were made there, and messages were passed there. Several Icelandic sagas follow this pattern. The phrase thin places for such spots is modern. The habit underneath is older. Showing up on time at the edge was the offering. Think of each edge as a meeting you keep.",
  "Try three dawns or three dusks in a row. Sit at a window or on a step for ten minutes. Do not rush. Notice the light and count three sounds. If cloud hides the sun, keep the same hour anyway. By the third day, the change in light should be easier to notice.",
  "Some old northern and Old English texts blame certain sicknesses on outside beings. The northern books name álfar (alfar), elves, and mara, a night-presser who sits on sleepers. Their touch is said to weigh on the body. This appears in Icelandic sagas and in Old English charms. Old English texts use ælfe (aelfe) for stitch and fever, and mære (maere) for the night mare. This appears in Old English leechbooks and charms. This book takes only the habit of watching the edges. It does not diagnose or treat illness. If you are unwell, see a clinician.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Meetings at edges appear in Icelandic sagas as a story pattern. The phrase “thin places” is modern."],
 "Choose dawn or dusk. Keep it three days in a row for ten minutes at the same window or step. Write light and three sounds after each sit. Give it three dated lines."),
("journal", "Liminal Places", "Well visit: go, tidy, listen. Write the date, place, what you tidied, what you heard, and what you left.", 12),
("journal", "Liminal Places", "Sea-road worksheet: what you are crossing, what you carry over, what you set down mid-water.", 12),
("lore", "Liminal Places", "The doorway rite",
 ["Spend four minutes at the door you use most. Do this once a month or at each turning of the seasons. Sweep the sill down to the bare step. Put fresh salt where the step meets the street. Hang a store-bought juniper sprig above the lintel, or store-bought rosemary if juniper is not on hand. Take a breath inside, then say one verse as you step out. Use store-bought herbs only, and do not eat or drink them. This ritual (the same steps in the same order each time) takes care, not fuss.",
  "Each part comes from everyday household habits in folk custom, not from one old book. Sweeping clears dust out of the doorway. Salt marks the line between inside and out. Green over the lintel is a sign of care for the house. A verse going out and thanks coming home bracket the day. The monthly schedule is modern, and this book says so. Nothing is burned or buried. The broom does much of the work before any words are spoken.",
  "When you come home, pause in the doorway for one breath and say one thank-you. This closes the going out. Four minutes covers both going and coming. Missing a month does not undo anything. Start again with the sweep, salt, green, and verse, with no penalty. A door kept this way helps the people inside feel settled. Keep the sweep and the words simple.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The threshold habits come from folk custom. The monthly schedule was written for this book."],
 "Write your going-out verse and your coming-home thanks tonight. Give sill, salt, green, and both lines. Keep them ready in four dated lines."),
("journal", "Liminal Places", "Three-day sit log: dawn or dusk, for three days. Write the light, the sounds, and what changed. Date each day.", 12),
("lore", "Runes", "Runes cut on stone",
 ["Run your finger along a stone’s cuts in low light and you feel the shape before you read it. A rune is a letter of the old runic alphabet. The Elder row, the older set in order, has twenty-four runes. They are cut on spearheads, on gold bracteates (thin gold medallions), and on standing stones, from roughly the second to the eighth century. The row begins fehu-uruz-þurisaz-ansuz-raidō-kenaz. Those six runes give the row its name, futhark, which is taken from its first six letters. The Kylver stone on Gotland carries the whole Elder row in order. Centuries later, the Younger row uses sixteen signs, in short-twig and long-branch forms. It was cut from roughly the ninth to the twelfth century on stones such as Rök and Jelling.",
  "The two rows differ in length, not in purpose. The Elder row spells things out fully. Its twenty-four runes are grouped into three families of eight. Later books call them the kin of Freyr, Hagall, and Týr. The Younger row shortens the writing to sixteen signs and leaves more to the reader. Stones give family names and memories. They say a stone was raised for a father. They never give spells or fortunes. The medieval rune poems (Norwegian, Icelandic, and Anglo-Saxon) pair each rune with a short saying. Wealth must move. Ice gives no grip. A torch shows the way. Read each saying slowly, one at a time.",
  "Your hands can learn the runes before your mind does. The first cuts were made by hands holding chisels, and hands still learn them best. Draw straight lines with a ruler, say each name aloud, and count the branches on each rune. Using your hand and your voice together helps the shapes stick. If the whole row feels like too much, start with the first six runes.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The rows are cut on stones from the 2nd to the 12th century, and the rune poems give the verses. The study practice is modern."],
 "Copy the Elder row one evening and the Younger row the next, using a ruler for straight lines. Sound each name and count three branches. Write which row your hand prefers and where the runes match. Give it four dated lines."),
("lore", "Runes", "Seven runes for your journal",
 ["This book uses seven short runes. Each rune has a saying that the medieval rune poems give. Fehu is cattle and wealth, which must move to stay alive. Uruz is the strong aurochs, a wild ox, pulling through mud. Þurisaz (Thurisaz) is the thorn that guards by pricking whoever grips it. Ansuz is the mouth of Óðinn, speaking counsel. Raidō (Raidho) is the road and riding, which can be smooth or hard on the feet. Kenaz is the torch that shows the evening work. Hagalaz is the hail that breaks roofs and bows to no one. Say each name aloud. Each rune fits a small slip of paper with its name on one side and its saying on the other.",
  "These rune names and sayings come from the medieval rune poems. The Norwegian, Icelandic, and Anglo-Saxon rune poems each speak in their own voice. Everything else in this practice is modern, and this book says so. The book does not claim that Viking Age people cast runes like this. The set is narrowed to seven because it suits a journal rather than a temple. The three-rune spread is modern. It sets out root, trunk, and branch from left to right.",
  "Ask one true question and boil it down to a single line. Lay three slips from left to right for root, trunk, and branch. The root shows what the matter rests on. The trunk shows what is steady. The branch shows what may grow. Ten minutes with pen and slips is enough. Write the date at the top of the page. Keep every cast in the same notebook pages across the months. Casts can show patterns that one night cannot.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The seven meanings are from the medieval rune poems (Norwegian, Icelandic, Anglo-Saxon). The narrowed set and the three-rune spread were written for this book."],
 "Boil one true question to a single line tonight. Draw three runes for root, trunk, and branch. Date the cast and write three plain sentences. Keep every cast in the same pages."),
("lore", "Staves", "Galdrastaves are late; use them honestly", ["Galdrastaves are late. They are not Viking Age runes. A galdrastave is a magic sign drawn in a charm book (a book of spells and charms). The earliest bound staves in Iceland appear in AM 434 a 12mo, an Icelandic charm book of the late 1400s. They were drawn beside remedies. The books many people know are early modern. Galdrabók (Galdrabok) dates to the 17th century. Lbs 4375 8vo dates to the 1860s. They mix a Christian frame, with Latin, runes, and pictures together. Ægishjálmr (Aegishjalmr), Helm of Awe, has old poetry behind it. Vegvísir (Vegvisir), the way-pointer, dates to the 1860s. It appears in the Huld manuscript, a 19th-century Icelandic collection. It is not a Viking Age sign.", "They stand as early modern folk practice, not Iron Age practice. In books such as Galdrabók, their power comes from calling on Christ, Mary, and angels alongside older names. They were drawn on paper or wood and carried. Calling them Viking amulets (lucky charms worn on the body) is a modern label. Kept in their own period, they stay accurate.", "Draw one stave on paper this week. Choose Ægishjálmr or a stave from AM 434 a 12mo, and write the date, book, and purpose beside it. Carry the paper for a day. Then bury it in soil or recycle it, with thanks. Use paper only, not skin, in this book.", "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The staves come from AM 434 a, Galdrabók (17th century), and the Huld manuscript (1860s), all late. This book leaves out any Viking Age use."], "Copy one stave on paper this week. Note the date, the book, and the purpose. Carry it for a day, then bury it in soil or recycle it. Log what held in three dated lines."),
("journal", "Staves", "Draw your valknútr (a sign of three linked triangles): trace the threes that matter to you, such as breath, blood, bone, or your own.", 10),
("lore", "Symbols", "Valknútr (Valknut) is old; the rest is newer",
 ["The valknútr (knot of the slain) is three triangles locked together. It is cut on Viking Age picture stones beside riders and ships. Its link to Óðinn is only suggested, by the scenes it appears in, not by a written label. Many people today read it as a sign of threes that matter, such as breath, blood, and bone, or three of your own choosing. Some carved stones are worn but plain, and their shapes can be hard to read. Trace the turn with your hand first. Your hand learns it faster than your eyes.",
  "The other marks belong to other places and times. The triquetra (a knot of three pointed loops) belongs to Early Christian and medieval art. Most tangled knotwork sold as a Viking badge is either medieval or modern craft. Enjoy them as craft. Called Viking, they mislead. Of the signs in this chapter, only the valknútr is Viking Age. The rest is newer. Loving them as craft takes nothing away.",
  "Sketch a valknútr on paper first, showing three triangles interlaced. Note the date each time. Next, draw it in chalk on your own step, and score (cut shallow lines in) a loaf of bread before baking. Each takes under half an hour. Note where each mark went and what washed away. Practice a little at a time until your wrist knows the turn.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The valknútr is cut on Viking Age picture stones. The triquetra comes from Christian and medieval art."],
 "Describe your valknútr in words, then draw it. Note which way its points turn and where it went. Give paper, chalk, and bread dates in three dated lines."),
("tracker", "Symbols", "Draw and mark tracker: where did your valknútr go this month?",
 ["Paper copy — date", "Doorway chalk — date", "Bread scored — date", "Stitched or drawn on cloth — date", "Garden or path marking — date", "Gift given with the mark — date", "What held, what washed away?"]),
]

CH8 = [
('lore', 'Charms', 'Galdr: the sung charm', ['A charm is a short set of words said or sung for a purpose. Óðinn in the Hávamál lists the songs he knows, the way a carpenter lists tools. Each one has its work named. The Hávamál says one song blunts an enemy’s blade. Another loosens chains from the wrists. Another calms a storm for rowers. Another wakes courage in tired men. The Sigrdrífumál in the Poetic Edda shows the same pattern in a smaller way. Brynhildr teaches Sigurðr rune songs for sword, ale, and healing. A galdr is a charm that is sung or spoken aloud, not one thought in silence. The voice does the work.', 'The voice is the teaching. The tune is not recorded. No melody survives in the Poetic Edda. The Hávamál gives purposes and boasts. It never gives notes on a staff. The medieval rune poems give names and meanings. They never give a music book. Any modern galdr with set tunes, robes, and staged circle work is a modern rebuilding. It is honest when called so. What the sources show is plainer. It is a verse learned by heart, said aloud, and said with care.', 'One verse learned by heart and spoken aloud in a low voice keeps the old shape. Speak it plainly at the same window three mornings in a row, without strain. A sung charm here means practiced words that calm your nerves, not weather you command. Keep your voice steady and unforced.', '<b>Where this comes from:</b> The charm list is in the Hávamál and the rune counsel is in the Sigrdrífumál, both in the Poetic Edda. No tunes survive, so any modern tune is rebuilt.'], 'Name the verse you will learn by heart. Speak it at the same window three mornings. Write the date, the verse, and what steadied you. Give it three dated lines.'),
("lore", "Charms", "Words in old books",
 ["Old northern voices survive in plain lines inside physician’s handbooks and the Old English leechbooks (old medical handbooks). The Merseburg-type charms from mainland Europe are close cousins in language. They speak healing over bone and blood. They say joint to joint, blood to blood.",
  "Surviving northern words lean toward protection and sometimes healing. This chapter follows only the protective side. Four workings are here, and each one protects or blesses. To ward means to guard. There is a walk around the room, a knot for the road, a bundle for the door, and a blessing of water. Nothing here curses or binds another person. Keep any words short enough to remember.",
  "Cangemi’s PhD thesis (Luthien Cangemi, UCL, 2024) gathers the spoken healing charms into four shapes that return in the Old English leechbooks and the Merseburg-type charms. One is a blessing spoken over the sick. One is a firm command that tells illness to leave. One is a holy story told as an example. One is a prayer said alongside the words. Any speaking in this book stays rebuilt household protection only. It is never a cure or a prescription. If you are ill, see a clinician.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Merseburg-type charms and the Old English leechbooks are old written sources."],
 "Name one part of your life that needs guarding this week. Write it in one line with no defeating and no fixing. Pin it where you work. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Charms", "Circling and cleansing",
 ["Walking a ring around a person or place, with your feet and your voice, is an old form of protection. The walk is called circling. The turn runs sunwise, meaning clockwise in the northern world, across northern custom. You walk around the person or place with a blessing until the circle closes. Beside it stands the body cleansing (a blessing with juniper or water). It cleanses the body part by part, as if dressing for the day. End with thanks to close the ring. Carl-Herman Tillhagen, a Swedish folk-custom collector, recorded Swedish farm protection customs as folk practice, so it is a sound source.",
  "Both shapes come down through a few old written hints and everyday folk practice. They appear across prayers and custom and were not invented here. The meaning for a home is simple. The ring says this ground is watched. The cleansing says this body is dressed for the day. A hallway, a garden bed, and a bedside all suit the ring. Your own words work well before sleep. Having someone walk the ring with you makes it more meaningful.",
  "Walk one small circle around a single room. Say a short line of protection and close with thanks at the door. Keep it under twenty minutes. For the cleansing, say six short lines, one for each part from head to foot, and touch each part as you say it. If you use juniper, use store-bought sprigs only, and do not eat or steep them. Repeat it often, and the habit will feel easier.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Circling and cleansing come from folk custom. Tillhagen recorded Swedish farm customs."],
 "Walk one circling tonight around a single room. Speak plain protection and close with thanks. Write your six-line cleansing from head to foot. Log where you walked in three dated lines."),
("lore", "Charms", "First working: the knot charm",
 ["You need a length of wool or linen cord. Folk custom across many districts ties protection into knots. Tie three knots slowly. Say one short blessing line over each knot as you pull it snug. The first knot is for the body, the second for the home, and the third for the road. Say each line aloud on your out-breath as you tighten the knot. Three knots and three lines make one charm.",
  "Knot-tying for protection is folk survival. It comes from many households, not one ritual. This renewal habit is modern practice built on that custom. A charm that is never checked can fade, so the practice includes renewal. At each turning of the season, untie the knots and tie them fresh, saying the lines anew. Retying is part of the care. Write a date on a tag or in the margin, so you know when the next turn is due.",
  "Make each line short enough to say in one breath. Tie the cord at a table. Then hang it high, near a draughty window or door, where daily brushing will not wear it thin. The work takes about twenty minutes with hands and voice. When the cord frays, wash it and tie it again. If the cord feels damp, let it dry before you hang it. Check the knots each time you walk past, and retie any that have come loose.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Knot-tying for protection comes from folk custom."],
 "Draft three short knot lines, each brief enough for one breath. Tie your cord tonight and hang it. Write lines, place, and date. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Charms", "Second working: the doorway bundle",
 ["A small bundle above the lintel holds household names. Tie a store-bought juniper twig and a store-bought rowan twig together with string. Rest a pinch of salt in a scrap of cloth. Write the names of the people under the roof on paper. Hang the bundle above the lintel of the door you use most, where you will see it each day. Keep the binding plain and the knot firm. Skip decoration. A thimbleful of salt is plenty. It is a token, not a supply.",
  "Juniper at the threshold, salt at the sill, and green over the door appear in folk custom. They come from many households, not one old recipe. Seasonal renewal is a modern habit with an older feel. At each turn of the season, fresh salt and fresh green go back in, with names spoken aloud as you set them back. Clearing out the old with care matters as much as hanging the new. Each name you put back inside keeps its place.",
  "The bundle takes half an hour with string and shop supplies. At renewal, pour old salt onto bare soil away from plants and paths. Never throw it in the bin beside the door. Put old green into soil or running water with thanks. Never bin it. Clear the old bundle with thanks before you make a new one.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The doorway ingredients come from folk custom."],
 "Make your bundle in half an hour. List contents and names in your journal tonight. Renew with fresh salt and green at the turning. Write all in three dated lines."),
("lore", "Charms", "Third working: the water blessing",
 ["Clean water in a small bottle brings a little of the well indoors. Draw it from your own tap with thanks, or from a well where you asked leave and took only a little. Keep it sealed apart for blessings, and label it not for drinking. Flick drops sunwise around a room, across the threshold, or onto your brow. This follows an old pattern called aspersion, which means sprinkling. It is a small household copy of well custom. Keep the bottle high and away from drinking vessels and small hands.",
  "This working is reconstructed from well custom, not copied from one ritual. It keeps the habits of asking leave, taking little, and turning sunwise. The rule is to use little. Never drink from wild water. Never fill a bottle from a protected spring. Never take more than the blessing needs. Change the water each month. Pour the old water onto plant roots, so nothing is wasted or harmed. Little and often is the whole method. Drops, not drinks.",
  "Keep the bottle near a spot you use every day, such as your desk or your bedroom door. Opening, flicking, thanking, and closing takes minutes. If you are unsure where to start, use the door you use most, since it needs the most care. A little water given often keeps the house feeling cared for.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The water blessing was rebuilt from well custom."],
 "Choose where your bottle will live and what it blesses first. Bless once and log date and place. Renew when the month turns. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Charms", "Uncrossing and repair",
 ["Charms can wear out, tangle, or get dirty. That is a sign they need care, not a sign of failure. Folk custom expects renewal, the way a house expects dusting. Uncrossing means taking the working apart gently. Knots are untied and cords washed. Bundles are emptied and sills swept. Water is poured out and bottles scrubbed. Name what slipped without shame and without hurry. Work in daylight with the windows open. Taking things apart feels like tidying, because it is.",
  "The remade working should be one step steadier than before. Keep the new parts simple, so they are easy to check next time. Give the new working a week before you change it again. Use a stronger line on the knot, a neater round at the door, and walk the circle once a week for a while instead of monthly. The renewal pattern is reconstructed from custom. The repair shape is modern and called so. Mending matters more than a perfect first try.",
  "Pick one charm that feels worn. Spend twenty minutes to uncross, clean, and remake it. Write down what slipped and what steadies it. Do this in a calm moment, not in a rush. Close with thanks. Notice how the house feels the next morning and write it down. Write the date beside the new work, so you can see how long it lasts.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The renewal pattern was rebuilt from custom. The repair steps were written for this book."],
 "Find one working that needs it tonight. Uncross, clean, remake in twenty minutes. Write what slipped in one line and what steadies it in the next. Give it three dated lines."),
("journal", "Charms", "Charm log: write which working you made, the date, and who it was for. Record all three.", 12),
("tracker", "Charms", "Home ward tracker, week one: walk the circling and mark each day.",
 ["Day 1 — walked?", "Day 2 — walked?", "Day 3 — walked?", "Day 4 — walked?", "Day 5 — walked?", "Day 6 — walked?", "Day 7 — walked + knots checked?", "What held this week?"]),
("tracker", "Charms", "Home ward tracker, week two: walk the circling and mark each day.",
 ["Day 8 — walked?", "Day 9 — walked?", "Day 10 — walked?", "Day 11 — walked?", "Day 12 — walked?", "Day 13 — walked?", "Day 14 — walked + bundle checked?", "What held across both weeks?"]),
]

CH9 = [
("lore", "Ancestors", "Mound-neighbors",
 ["Low green mounds in the fields are old burial places. Haugr, the burial mound, stands in Icelandic sagas as the home of the dead. In those sagas the dead keep watch over cattle and weather. People greeted them with toasts poured at the door-stone and kept their paths swept. Egils saga (Egil’s Saga) and other Icelandic sagas show gifts laid and words spoken at mounds. This book does not try to wake them. In this telling, your people are not far off. They are neighbors near dusk, close enough to be remembered at the table.",
  "This closeness is gentle. It is for comfort, not proof. Mounds and ship-settings (stone outlines shaped like ships) are found by archaeologists, the people who dig up and study past sites. You can visit them. The talking dead come from Icelandic sagas. That is a story pattern, not a map. Nothing here asks you to prove or perform anything. One shared meal a year is enough. A card, a letter, or a list of names also works. These are small ways to remember. Grief is welcome here, and so are good manners.",
  "A name can be honored with no ritual at all. Light a candle, speak a name, and remember one true thing. That is all it takes. Set the candle on a steady, flat surface, away from curtains and paper. Never leave a flame unattended. Put the candle out before bed. Fuller remembrance is on the supper page when you are ready. There is no test. Speak a name and be kind.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Mounds appear in Icelandic sagas and in archaeology."],
 "Rest with one name tonight. Light a candle if you like, speak the name aloud, and never leave it burning unattended. Write one true sentence before sleep. Give it three dated lines and the time you put the candle out."),
("lore", "Ancestors", "The quiet supper script",
 ["This supper sets one more plate than the living need. The first half of the meal is eaten in silence. Serve a simple, hot meal. Soup, bread, or a dish they used to make can be the food. Light a candle for each name you will speak. Keep an empty chair for them. Eat together in quiet, and the dead share the hour with you.",
  "Halfway through, speak each name aloud with one true sentence. Offer a thanks, a memory, or something you never said. Then eat the second half while keeping them in mind. The shape comes down through folk custom. Many households kept it. This script is one gentle modern version. It is meant for Yule or Winter Nights. Once a year is plenty. Steady remembrance matters more than grandeur. Tears are fine.",
  "After the meal, close with thanks and put out the candles. Never leave a candle burning when you leave the room. Leave a little food out for birds or neighbors in the morning. The whole practice fits in an hour, not counting the dishes. Leave the names by the bed until morning.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The quiet supper comes from folk custom, with hints in Icelandic sagas."],
 "Choose the menu and names tonight. Keep the supper inside an hour at Yule or Winter Nights. Write the menu, names, and morning notes. Give it three dated lines."),
("journal", "Ancestors", "Ancestor card, first: write the name, dates, one story, one thing they left you, and one question.", 13),
("journal", "Ancestors", "Ancestor card, second: write the name, dates, one story, one thing they left you, and one question.", 13),
("journal", "Ancestors", "Grief letter and roll call: write a letter you will not send, then list the names you will speak at Yule.", 13),
]

CH10 = [
("journal", "Oath", "Your vow page: fill it in. I will keep ___ for 30 days. I will not ___. Witnessed by ___.", 12),
("resources", "Resources", "Books behind this book"),
("lore", "Oath", "Go on reading",
 ["Four old texts go further into this subject. Read small parts in public translations before you buy anything. The Poetic Edda in Larrington’s translation opens with the seeress and the counsel of Hávamál. Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda in Faulkes’s version tells the stories plainly. The Völsunga Saga in Byock’s translation follows Sigurðr to the pyre. Egils saga tells of a poem that buys back Egil’s own head in York. Borrow them first, so you can judge them fairly.",
  "Read these books first as stories, and second as lessons. They are original old texts, in translation, not summaries. They reward slow reading. For each part, ask what happened, what it means at home, and what small act answers it. Let the answer settle over a week. A chapter a week is a good pace. Keep a notebook beside you, and write your answers there.",
  "Start with one book and one starting day. The next morning, sit in the same spot and read the next section. An hour or less per sitting is plenty. Pick a time that suits you and keep it. When you finish, pass the book on, and mark the page that held you longest. Keeping to your reading plan is a small vow you can keep.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The primary texts to read next. Go meet them."],
 "Commit to one book and one starting day. Begin the next morning in the same spot. Write book, day, and the page that holds you longest. Give it three dated lines."),
]
